--TEST--
`Core\Test::expectFailure` requires its callable to fail and discharges that failure, and refuses a callable that does not -- `rule:testing/failure-ledger`
--FILE--
<?nvs
// § 5's second half. The ledger itself is state a program cannot reach, so
// what a case can observe is the member that discharges from it: which bodies
// it accepts, which it refuses, and that the refusal is the same
// `Core\Test\Failure` an assertion raises.

use Core\Test;

int $agreed = 0;

// 1. The shape the section exists for: a helper consuming a failure it meant
// to provoke. Nothing is printed and nothing escapes. Written out in full
// here and through the `use` below, which are one member either way.
Core\Test::expectFailure(fn (): void => { Core\Test::assertSame(1, 2); });
echo "a provoked failure is consumed\n";

// 2. A body that fails nothing is itself a failure -- which is the half that
// keeps `expectFailure` from being a way to say "run this and shrug".
try {
    Test::expectFailure(fn (): void => { Test::assertSame(1, 1); });
} catch (Core\Test\Failure $none) {
    echo "no failure: ", $none->message, "\n";
    $agreed = $agreed + 1;
}

// 3. The question asked is what the ledger says, not what class came back: a
// body that caught its own failure still failed. That is exactly the
// silently-passing test § 5 abolishes, seen from the other side.
Test::expectFailure(fn (): void => {
    try {
        Test::assertEquals("a", "b");
    } catch (Throwable $swallowed) {
    }
});
echo "a swallowed failure is still a failure\n";

// 4. Each of § 4's three members reaches it, one throw site being what they
// share.
Test::expectFailure(fn (): void => { Test::assertSame(1, 2); });
$agreed = $agreed + 1;
Test::expectFailure(fn (): void => { Test::assertEquals("x", "y"); });
$agreed = $agreed + 1;
Test::expectFailure(fn (): void => {
    array<int> $actual = [1];
    array<int> $expected = [2];
    Test::assertEqualsDeep($actual, $expected);
});
$agreed = $agreed + 1;
echo "every member's failure is expectable\n";

// 5. Nesting: the inner call discharges its own body's failure, so the outer
// one sees none of its own and refuses.
try {
    Test::expectFailure(fn (): void => {
        Test::expectFailure(fn (): void => { Test::assertSame(1, 2); });
    });
} catch (Core\Test\Failure $nested) {
    echo "nested: ", $nested->message, "\n";
    $agreed = $agreed + 1;
}

// 6. A passing assertion beside the failing one is untouched by the discharge,
// so the body may do real work either side of the failure it provokes.
Test::expectFailure(fn (): void => {
    Test::assertSame(3, 3);
    Test::assertSame(3, 4);
});
echo "a passing assertion beside it survives\n";
$agreed = $agreed + 1;

echo "agreed=", $agreed, "/6\n";
--EXPECT--
a provoked failure is consumed
no failure: Core\Test::expectFailure failed: the callable ran without a failed assertion
a swallowed failure is still a failure
every member's failure is expectable
nested: Core\Test::expectFailure failed: the callable ran without a failed assertion
a passing assertion beside it survives
agreed=6/6
